1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
give in to that person and just accept it and be happy, I'd rather die.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:13,000
So, again, maybe this is all exaggeration, but it seems like it's part of the human condition.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:17,000
To me, anyway, you can make up your own mind about whether I'm making this up or whether

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
it seems to describe you or some other people you've ever encountered.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Anyway, so I'll say you can normally pay $100 for something without having any idea how

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:31,000
happy it brings me. Here, one of my favorite examples is granite in kitchens.

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:38,000
This stuff costs a lot of money, and yet I've never seen any owner of granite smile at the granite,

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,000
talk to the granite, and say, oh, this granite, I love this granite, it's so good.

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,000
I've never seen anything produce the slightest sign of happiness, and there's a, like, smiles,

10
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,000
a tear in your eye of joy. No, the ownership of granite gives a very different emotion

11
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,000
from what I would call happiness. On the other hand, how often have you seen ice cream

12
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
met with anything other than a tear of joy, like an ice cream cone, mmm!

13
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:12,000
That $1 of ice cream brought more happiness than that $50,000 slab of granite ever will.

14
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Didn't it? Maybe that's how it seems to me.

15
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
And then you can know something makes me very happy without knowing, or even if I have a

16
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,000
little illness, pay for it. So, like a lot of people would not pay their children to say I love you.

17
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
I would not, I wouldn't, like, how much would you pay your child to say I love you?

18
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,000
I wouldn't pay them to say that. But how happy does it make you? Really happy.

19
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:39,000
I love you, daddy. Aww. How much would I pay for that? I'm not going to pay for it, but it's great.

20
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,000
So there is a disconnect. Is there a correlation? Sure, but they're different concepts.

21
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Utilitarianism is exactly about maximizing utility or pleasure or happiness.

22
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,000
There's many different variations on it because it is a topic that has provided employment

23
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,000
for thousands of philosophers over the last 150 years.

24
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Alright. So, anyway, it is about that.

25
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:11,000
A lot of economists have, in the 20th century, the philosophical problem of utilitarianism,

26
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,000
which is, look, you can't measure happiness. How can you possibly do it?

27
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,000
And that's what necessary, you have to make an interpersonal utility comparison.

28
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
You have to say, this person is happier than that person.

29
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,000
And where's the happy-o-meter that would let you do that, or the util-o-meter?

30
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,000
And this leaves many economists to say, utilitarianism is actually meaningless,

31
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,000
we can't talk about it, not possible.

32
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Or, how do you add up happiness?

33
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Alright. Now my view is this is an argument that sounds really good,

34
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,000
again, until you actually pay any attention to the real world.

35
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
And notice, look, how can we do it? Well, everybody does it, so it must be doable.

36
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Any time you ever said one friend was happier than another friend,

37
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,000
you just did an interpersonal utility comparison, didn't you?

38
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
What would your life be like if you just stopped ever doing that again?

39
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
And suddenly, you know, who's happier? Who's happiness in this room?

40
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Can't say. It's not an answerable question. It's a meaningless question.

41
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
One person is a giant grin, everyone else is crying,

42
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,000
you can't tell me who's the happiest person in this room?

43
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Seems like you could.

44
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
So, it may be hard, there may be cases where it's hard to figure out.

45
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Maybe someone is carrying a great deal of sadness that they hide.

46
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,000
But, that's different from saying it's conceptually impossible or meaningless.

47
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:32,000
And here's the thing, once you say that you can't compare the happiness of two people,

48
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:37,000
doesn't that also mean that you can't compare the happiness of an individual over time?

49
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,000
Right? So, how can you say that I'm less happy now when I'm being tortured

50
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:45,000
than before, than yesterday when I was free?

51
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,000
If you can't say that the person who's being tortured is less happy than the person who's free,

52
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:55,000
how can you say that you can compare the happiness of one individual in two different situations?

53
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,000
So, again, that seems really weird to think that you can't do that.

54
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Would that be to say that an absolute utility comparison isn't possible,

55
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:12,000
but a relative, or kind of a, just not an exact?

56
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
You may say, well, it's hard being exact, sure, but,

57
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:20,000
it's basically saying there's no fundamental conceptual problem with this.

58
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Now, if you've ever argued with Walter Block, and if you ever have,

59
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
the argument will never stop until you let it give him the last word.

60
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,000
That's for you, Walter Block student over there.

61
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
We were talking about this. We said that exactly.

62
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:39,000
Well, to be fair, Walter does have a whole article where he argues that the best standard of who's right

63
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:44,000
is whoever has the last word, and he really does, and then he goes and he actually applies it

64
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:49,000
empirically to about 50 different disputes, and he categorizes who got the last word,

65
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,000
and then goes and ranks it and says so.

66
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Turns out that Austrians are more right empirically,

67
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:59,000
because we, and often he personally, made sure to have the last word.

68
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:06,000
So, he has a view, but I think it's far from being an idiosyncratic view that just says,

69
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:09,000
look, there's two different ways of talking.

70
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,000
There's the scientific way, and there's the normal way,

71
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:17,000
and scientifically, it is impossible to go and ever say that something raises overall happiness,

72
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,000
or makes an individual happy, or one happy individual is happier than another person,

73
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,000
but of course we can do it in a normal way.

74
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,000
My question for him is always, alright, well, is the normal way,

75
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,000
when you're doing it in the normal way, is it still true?

76
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,000
In that case, why don't we just talk the normal way?

77
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
And yet, if it's not still true, then you haven't really solved anything.

78
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
You've just bitten the bullet and just say yes,

79
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
and then you're just, it's just a metaphor to say that the person being tortured is less happy

80
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
than the person who's been free.

81
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:49,000
That seems weird to me.

82
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Yes?

83
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:56,000
At the risk of assuming two people, it would be as if you had two separate planets

84
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,000
that could contact each other, or two separate countries even.

85
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,000
Let's say you took planets, two different life forms with different biological means,

86
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:05,000
and each planet operated on a one world currency.

87
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,000
You could never compare the currency values of each one,

88
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:11,000
because there was no possible commensurable thing.

89
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
You could track the value of the currency over time.

90
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,000
You could track the value of the global dollar versus the Mars coin,

91
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:21,000
but you could never say how many global dollars does the Mars coin worth,

92
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:25,000
because nothing on Mars is something that we'd ever buy.

93
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Right, of course you could still make a guess about what the exchange rate would be like

94
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
if there were an exchange rate.

95
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,000
So, conceptually it's still doable, and in fact,

96
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:38,000
say look, I predict that if exchange ever opens up, that this is what the rate's going to be.

97
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:44,000
And it seems meaningful, it may be that there's no way that it's never going to get decisively resolved,

98
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:50,000
just like if you were to say, like if Klaus von Schaufenberg successfully assassinated Hitler,

99
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:55,000
then the borders of Germany at the end of World War II would have wound up being very different.

100
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:59,000
There's no way of ever checking that, yet it seems true.

101
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,000
Of course you've never, maybe you've never heard of Schaufenberg in which case, I don't know.

102
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:09,000
But most people who study the facts would say, yeah, it seems pretty likely.

103
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
All right.

104
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,000
Now, let's see.

105
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:19,000
Now, one common thing for economists to say is, well, utilitarianism and Calgary's are almost the same.

106
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:25,000
All that we're doing here is that utilitarianism is adjusting for the marginal utility of wealth.

107
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,000
So, common thing to say.

108
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:32,000
So, there's something to this, but it's hardly necessarily true.

109
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,000
People might be willing to pay for things other than happiness,

110
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:39,000
or there might be some people who just get very little happiness out of a dollar.

111
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:43,000
Like, you know anyone who just seems to not really enjoy much of anything?

112
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:46,000
And there's other people who just savor every experience.

113
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:52,000
So, yeah, I mean, so, like, again, if you were utilitarian, you wouldn't just stop at redistribution from rich to poor.

114
00:07:52,000 --> 00:08:00,000
You might also say, let's also redistribute from people who have very unresponsive emotions

115
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
to people who have very responsive emotions.

116
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:07,000
Let's go and take stuff from people who seem to be the same regardless of what happens to them

117
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:14,000
and give it to people who have, like, who really appreciate things or really dislike poverty.

118
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:19,000
So, like, you know, probably, like, you know, cheerful people would get bigger redistribution.

119
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:23,000
And the chronically miserable people, well, like, they're always going to be miserable, so what's the difference?

120
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,000
Let's grow them blind.

121
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:27,000
Right?

122
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:32,000
Seems reasonable, and you probably have some friends that just seem miserable all the time.

123
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:36,000
And then at some point you may say, why do I try to cheer you up?

124
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:37,000
Nothing works.

125
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:42,000
I'm going to go and put my energy into someone that does get cheered up when I do things for them.

126
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:43,000
All right.

127
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:47,000
And, of course, utilitarianism is often used to justify redistribution.

128
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:51,000
But, you know, even on its own terms, there's problems with this.

129
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:57,000
And then there is a classic philosopher's counter example, which is fun.

130
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:00,000
I'm never going to mention it again, but you might want to hear about it.

131
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,000
It's the utility monster.

132
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:05,000
This is a genetically engineered creature with increasing marginal utility wealth.

133
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:09,000
So every time you double its wealth, it gets more than a doubling of happiness.

134
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:13,000
And all the other creatures in the universe have diminishing marginal utility wealth.

135
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:20,000
So it's easy to come up with a story where actually the most efficient and the most utilitarian thing to do

136
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:25,000
is to expropriate everyone else on Earth or in the universe, give it all to the utility monster.

137
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
The rest of us die in horrible agony.

138
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:28,000
But don't worry.

139
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:35,000
That utility monster loves all your possessions so much that it's better, in utility terms, that we're all dead.

140
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:39,000
And then you have plenty of other oddities here.

141
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:46,000
So there's a long-running debate between average and total utilitarianism.

142
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:50,000
Many people don't like the idea of total utilitarianism.

143
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:58,000
It means that if you create a new life where the person is just barely better off than indifferent between being alive,

144
00:09:58,000 --> 00:09:59,000
that's a good thing.

145
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:02,000
So it's a good thing to create very marginal lives.

146
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
So many people say, no, that's crazy.

147
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:08,000
Then there's the average utilitarianism has even weirder implications.

148
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:11,000
So let's maximize the average utility in the world.

149
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:15,000
Well, does that mean that we should just kill off everyone except the happiest person on Earth?

150
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:18,000
What if he's a misanthrope so he doesn't get lonely?

151
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,000
And then, oh, this is great.

152
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
I've got a whole planet to myself.

153
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:23,000
I'm so happy.

154
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:27,000
Average utility would never have been this high when the world had other people on it.

155
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
But now they're gone.

156
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
Isn't that a Twilight Zone episode?

157
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
And then his glasses break.

158
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:34,000
Oh, yeah.

159
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:39,000
I mean, he wasn't so much a misanthrope as he just liked books a lot and everyone was mean to him.

160
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:43,000
He didn't actually revel in the deaths of others.

161
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
He was pretty excited to go to that library.

162
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:47,000
Yeah, he was.

163
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:51,000
But you wouldn't have minded if there were other bookworms he could hang out with.

164
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
He wouldn't have cared about that.

165
00:10:53,000 --> 00:11:02,000
Anyway, so I mention this just because there is an unusually high share of people at GMU who do economics of philosophy.

166
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:06,000
So if any of this stuff interests you, there is a lot you can do here.

167
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:14,000
It's actually likely you'll have a better career as being an economist who does some empirical work and also does some economics of philosophy after tenure.

168
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:18,000
Probably a way better career than if you just try to be a pure philosopher doing this stuff.

169
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:26,000
So this is sort of my general career advice to almost everyone is if you do math, just get a PhD in economics, get tenure in economics,

170
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:29,000
and then call whatever you do the economics of that.

171
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:36,000
And even if you never get another raise, you'll still do better than most history professors or sociologists or other people ever will.

172
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
So worth considering.

173
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:41,000
All right, so questions about utilitarianism?

174
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:42,000
Everyone happy?

175
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:43,000
All right, really happy.

176
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:48,000
Not just willing to pay, but a genuine emotion of well-being.

177
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:49,000
Okay.

178
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
All right, then moving on.

179
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:52,000
All right.

180
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:56,000
The comparative institution's first and second best.

181
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:00,000
All right, now I'm guessing that Walter Williams mentioned Harold Dempsetz to you.

182
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:01,000
More than once.

183
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:04,000
All right, yes, the great Harold Dempsetz.

184
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:07,000
All right, and Harold Dempsetz had a bunch of papers.

185
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:18,000
One of them, well, I think there's only one where he mentioned Buddhist theology, which was the paper on the Nirvana Fallacy.

186
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:24,000
Now, if someone actually understands Buddhism, I think we'll say that he has a total misunderstanding of it, but that's not important.

187
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
What's important is what he was talking about.

188
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:39,000
So what Dempsetz called the Nirvana Fallacy is doing what pretends to be Calgary-Hicks efficiency analysis while selectively relaxing whatever constraints you don't feel like worrying about.

189
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:45,000
In order to go, and then we hang it, why do you go and selectively ignore a bunch of constraints?

190
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:48,000
To get the answer you want, of course.

191
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:54,000
Again, of course, it may be that you do it because other people get mad at you if you have the wrong answer, so you do it that way.

192
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
Maybe you do it just because no one else ever did.

193
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
Maybe you are actually just subconscious.

194
00:12:58,000 --> 00:12:59,000
Maybe it's very conscious.

195
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
But anyway, this is what he talked about.

196
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:13,000
So you're supposedly doing this, but then you do a very sloppy job where you forget important constraints, and then you come up with an answer that doesn't make a lot of sense.

197
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:16,000
All right, now, why was he making this claim?

198
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:26,000
Well, at the time that Dempsetz was writing, there was something we now call old-style welfare economics where people just did really crummy Calgary-Hicks analysis

199
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:37,000
where they ignored a bunch of constraints, and it's fair to say that suspiciously ignoring the constraints generally tilted the scales heavily towards government regulation.

200
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:46,000
Right now, I'll say that when I was an undergraduate at UC Berkeley, Dempsetz's critique still applied wholly to most of the undergraduate classes.

201
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:47,000
Really.

202
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:55,000
So I can't tell you how many times I was in an undergraduate class where someone would just go and draw that triangle, draw the monopoly, show everybody an off-striangle,

203
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
and then say, see, government's got you.

204
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
Government must do something to fix this.

205
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:02,000
And this is what government will solve the problem.

206
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:11,000
And again, there's the ambiguity of in the real world or in this model, but the tone is always, well, yeah, like the real world.

207
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:17,000
Kind of dogmatic, who will argue that you don't see how these triangles show that government is good.

208
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:20,000
They're just geometrical shapes.

209
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:25,000
They don't really show anything in the real world, at least not until you do some more work.

210
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:33,000
So he was complaining about the shortcomings of government not being factored in and actually often just overhead not being figured in.

211
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:39,000
So when someone says we need regulation, at minimum you say, well, how much are the regulators going to cost?

212
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:40,000
Don't they need a building?

213
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
And what about their phone bills?

214
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
And just add up all that stuff.

215
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:45,000
At minimum, you've got to put that in.

216
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:53,000
Maybe all that stuff is bigger than the triangle, in which case, turns out it's not much of an argument for government regulation.

217
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
All right.

218
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:04,000
So, I'll say since 20 years after he wrote the article, I still saw this going on routinely at UC Berkeley, or at least classes for undergraduates.

219
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:07,000
I don't think this is a straw man at the time.

220
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:11,000
I think he actually is attacking a very common point of view.

221
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:15,000
So I have a blog post called The Straw Man, Straw Man.

222
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:22,000
And this is where people accuse you of attacking a straw man when in fact you're attacking an extremely popular argument.

223
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
And they're like, look, it's not a straw man.

224
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:25,000
Why doesn't he hold this?

225
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
So I'm criticizing a view that people actually hold.

226
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
How is that a straw man?

227
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:34,000
So a straw man is supposed to be an argument so stupid that virtually nobody holds it.

228
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:37,000
But there's a lot of popular stupid arguments.

229
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
So what's wrong with those?

230
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:40,000
Well, they're terrible.

231
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:41,000
But what's wrong with attacking them?

232
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
Nothing.

233
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:44,000
They should be attacked.

234
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:45,000
All right.

235
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:48,000
Now this is a classic example of what Zebby says he's talking about.

236
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:49,000
All right.

237
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:50,000
Price greater than marginal cost.

238
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
That's right here, right?

239
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:56,000
So remember, as I said, the monopolist produces a marginal cost equals marginal revenue.

240
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:00,000
And therefore, price is up here, marginal cost is down there, debt rate costs.

241
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:01,000
Terrible.

242
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:09,000
And I think that during my four years as an undergraduate, I saw maybe 500 debt rate loss triangles drawn.

243
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:16,000
And always these triangles were to show market's bad and government can, should, will, and must solve this problem.

244
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:19,000
And only a dogmatic fool would doubt it.

245
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:20,000
All right.

246
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:23,000
So it all comes down to this problem of price being greater than marginal cost.

247
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:26,000
Now what is the classic remedy for this?

248
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
It's a really simple one.

249
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
So you've got monopoly.

250
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:34,000
So I guess to me the simplest one is just trust busting down a perfect competition.

251
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
But there's something much simpler here.

252
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
Say, well, how about we just impose a price control?

253
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
Price equals marginal cost price control.

254
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:47,000
And in a monology, if you remember the way the monopoly model works, if you get that price control exactly right, it doesn't discourage production at all.

255
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:51,000
Because essentially the monopolist treats that like his new demand curve.

256
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:56,000
And then he just sets, you know, he produces until marginal cost equals that price.

257
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:59,000
Because the price control, the whole idea is that it's flat.

258
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:00,000
It's horizontal, right?

259
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
Just like a perfect competition.

260
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:03,000
All right.

261
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
So you go and you set this price control.

262
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:10,000
And then, and then you notice, and then the monopolist produces there, debt rate cost solved, and the problem.

263
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:11,000
All right.

264
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:14,000
So this is a classic Nirvana fallacy remedy.

265
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:15,000
All right.

266
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:19,000
Just think about all the problems of doing this in the real world.

267
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:20,000
All right.

268
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:30,000
Well, so, you know, they like to say, so, you know, the, this creates a secondary, the price control creates a secondary problem, which is, wait a second, why do we have a monopoly anyway?

269
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:33,000
Is this really a constant returns to scale industry that has a monopoly?

270
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:34,000
It's weird.

271
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:37,000
How often do you get monopolies in constant returns to scale industries?

272
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
Almost never.

273
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:40,000
All right.

274
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
So, and you say, yeah, yeah, well, yeah, okay, fine.

275
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:43,000
All right.

276
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:44,000
Well, yeah, there's some fixed costs.

277
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:45,000
So what?

278
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:54,000
Well, if you impose a constant, you know, you impose this, this, you know, you set pricing with a marginal cost, and there's fixed costs, then the firms are going to be losing money, right?

279
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:55,000
Normally.

280
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:56,000
Like, you can fiddle with it.

281
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:02,000
But normally, if there's a fixed cost and there's a constant marginal cost, you make the price a marginal cost, so actually that case always.

282
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:06,000
So if there's a fixed cost, constant marginal cost, you make the price a marginal cost, they will lose money.

283
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:07,000
Predictably.

284
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
They will lose the fixed cost.

285
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:10,000
All right.

286
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:19,000
So then you can say, well, you can't expect the industry to stay in business losing money forever, so then your great remedy isn't going to make the product available at marginal cost.

287
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,000
Instead, it's going to remove the product from the market.

288
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
Thanks a lot.

289
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:25,000
Right?

290
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:26,000
And, oh, well, come on.

291
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:27,000
All right.

292
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:28,000
Fine.

293
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
Well, we'll go and give them a subsidy in order to make up that fixed cost.

294
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:33,000
Let's go and hand them a bag of money from the fixed cost, just enough to keep them in business.

295
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:34,000
All right.

296
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:35,000
Now everything's great.

297
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:36,000
All right.

298
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:39,000
And then, all right, well, how are you going to fund these subsidies?

299
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:42,000
Where's the money for the subsidies coming from?

300
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:43,000
Oh, my God.

301
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:44,000
You're full of questions.

302
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
Why do I have to deal with all this?

303
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:51,000
Look, I have my brilliant remedy, and now you're going and asking me a bunch of questions like where's the money supposed to come from?

304
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:52,000
All right.

305
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
Well, of course, like, well, yes, you've caught us.

306
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
The money's going to come from taxes.

307
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
But wait a second.

308
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:03,000
Isn't the whole thing that taxes do create a gap between price and marginal cost?

309
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:12,000
So your solution to this problem of price being greater than marginal cost is ultimately to go and impose a tax, which then creates a gap between price and marginal cost.

310
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:18,000
Right now, in particularly obtuse theorists, they say, no, no, no, we're assuming they're all on some taxes.

311
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:19,000
All right.

312
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
And where are the role of those?

313
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:22,000
Nowhere.

314
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:29,000
So maybe you shouldn't have talked down to people so quickly before when you proposed your supposedly brilliant remedy to the problem.

315
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:33,000
And this is what Dem says was getting at.

316
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:34,000
All right.

317
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:35,000
Okay.

318
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:36,000
So questions?

319
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:37,000
Right.

320
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:43,000
Again, like, this error seems so basic to me that I know there's plenty of economists who'll say no one was that dumb.

321
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:50,000
Not only are there plenty of people that dumb, there are plenty of textbooks that are written this badly, and people teach undergraduate stuff.

322
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:58,000
And maybe there's no actual professor that really believes something that dumb, but if you're teaching it to undergraduates, then there's still a problem.

323
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:01,000
But I think if you're teaching it to undergraduates as if it is dumb, then there's a problem.


